+
+### 10.5 Words to use and words to avoid
+
+Say "AI-assisted intake" and "an AI-written, rule-verified plan". Never say "AI-driven advice", "compliant", "secure", "anonymous", "eligible", "approved", "guaranteed" or "the bank will lend". The loan and scheme figures are estimates, not offers, and eligibility is not checked. Do not call the translations reviewed. Do not say the engine was built this week. Do not call the app "voice-only": speech is on the optional `/talk` page, and the Listen button reads two sentences and the light. Say the Listen button "uses a voice on the device" and only if it was seen working in the browser you recorded in; never say it reads the figures. Do not present a screen from before the start as new: the corner label says which is which.
+
+### 10.6 If something fails while recording
+
+- **The microphone is blocked or the transcript is poor:** type the same sentence in the box, fix any mis-heard word, and press "Find my answers". The typed path still uses the real model.
+- **The helper says it is not available or busy:** wait a minute and retry. If it stays down, record the form-only flow (shots 4 to 6 work without the helper) and add the model shots from a later take.
+- **A plan comes back refused** ("A plan could not be written this time"): press "Write my plan" again. The checks refuse a reply with a digit, a link or the wrong script, and the page keeps the advice above it either way. A refusal is not a bug.
+- **The model puts a number in the wrong field:** a take that shows a swapped amount is not worth keeping; record it again.
+- **The advice page shows no loan cards, funding card or registrations card:** the display is on "Simple", which is where a clean browser starts and which the browser remembers. Press "Detailed" at the top of the page and retake the shot.
+
+### 10.7 Draft of the submission text
+
+अर्थNiti turns what a first-time borrower says into a plain answer: can this business afford a loan, which government schemes may apply, and what to do next. It is aimed at the lenders and field staff who sit with these borrowers. A person speaks or types a few sentences in their own language. Muse Voice Transcribe writes the words down and Muse Spark finds the form answers in them; every answer is shown with the person's own words beside it, and nothing enters the form until they have seen it. Everything after that is plain Python rules with no model: profit, repayment pressure and the household check, loan sizes with a month-by-month schedule, the schemes that match the work and the place, what each scheme's published terms put in and what they add, the registrations to ask about, and district prices where government or NECC data exists. A second, optional use of Muse Spark writes a short plan in the person's language around blanks; the code fills the blanks from the rules and refuses a plan that carries a number of its own, a link or the wrong script. Models do the language; rules do the money.
+
+Prior work, called out in `docs/HACKATHON.md` section 2: the rules engine, the scheme data and the interface were built before the event began, and so was the first version of the intake service (the Meta client and its two routes), written in the two hours before the 12:00 PM ET start. Built during it: the voice-and-text page with its recorder and review screen, the spend ledger that keeps the API use inside the free credits, the written plan, the loan options and repayment schedule, the funding card, the registrations card, a green, yellow or red light on the result pages (set by the rules, not by a model), a Simple or Detailed view of them, egg prices and market rows for 29 more districts. Limits, stated on the pages: no scheme's eligibility rules are checked, all figures are estimates and not offers, and the wording in the seven languages other than English is machine-written and has not been reviewed by native speakers. The video labels each screen as new or from before the event.
+
